---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { APK_URL, formatDate } from '../i18n';
import { releases } from '../releases';
import Screenshots from '../components/Screenshots.astro';

// The newest release, shown under the download button.
const [latest] = await releases('en');
---
<Page lang="en" page="index" title='CastBay: AirPlay Receiver for Android TV – Mirror Your iPhone' description='Free, open-source app that turns an Android TV or Google TV into an AirPlay receiver: mirror your iPhone, iPad or Mac, play music with lyrics, and cast video. DLNA too.'>
<main>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow slogan">Cast it, it's there. Kick back with CastBay.</p>
      <h1>
        Cast your iPhone to your Android TV
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">
        CastBay lets an Android TV receive AirPlay screen mirroring, music and video, and the cast button in video and music apps (DLNA). Free, open source, no ads.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={APK_URL}>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3v12m0 0-5-5m5 5 5-5M4 19h16"/></svg>Download APK
        </a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/weenas/castbay">View source
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Version {latest.data.version} · {formatDate('en', latest.data.date)} · <a href="/changelog">What's new</a><br>
        For Android TV / Google TV devices on Android 8.0 or later
      </p>
      <div class="chips">
        <span class="chip">Screen mirroring</span>
        <span class="chip">Music &amp; lyrics</span>
        <span class="chip">Video casting</span>
        <span class="chip">DLNA</span>
        <span class="chip">H.265 · 4K</span>
        <span class="chip">English &amp; Chinese</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="features">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Everything casting to a TV should be</h2>
        <p>Designed for the big screen and the remote: mirroring, music and video each get a screen made for them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        <article class="card">
          <div class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="2" y="4" width="20" height="13" rx="2"/><path d="M8 21h8M12 17v4"/></svg></div>
          <h3>Screen mirroring</h3>
          <p>Mirror an iPhone, iPad or Mac in real time at up to 60 fps, and in 4K with H.265 on 4K TVs with hardware HEVC decoding.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <div class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18V5l12-2v13"/><circle cx="6" cy="18" r="3"/><circle cx="18" cy="16" r="3"/></svg></div>
          <h3>Music</h3>
          <p>A blurred-cover backdrop, scrolling lyrics (optional) and round playback controls. AirPlay music plays in lossless ALAC, timed to the phone as on an Apple TV, so lyrics stay in sync.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <div class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="2" y="5" width="20" height="14" rx="2"/><path d="m10 9 5 3-5 3z"/></svg></div>
          <h3>Video casting</h3>
          <p>When an app casts a video, the TV plays it straight from the source, so your phone is free. Switch audio tracks and subtitles.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <div class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M2 8.5a15 15 0 0 1 20 0M5.5 12a10 10 0 0 1 13 0M9 15.5a5 5 0 0 1 6 0"/><circle cx="12" cy="19" r="1.2" fill="currentColor"/></svg></div>
          <h3>Apps' cast button (DLNA)</h3>
          <p>The cast button in apps like Bilibili, iQiyi, NetEase Cloud Music and QQ Music casts straight to CastBay, from iPhone and Android phones alike.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <div class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="7" y="2" width="10" height="20" rx="5"/><circle cx="12" cy="8" r="2"/><path d="M12 14v3"/></svg></div>
          <h3>Made for the remote</h3>
          <p>Press Down while playing for the quick menu: picture fit, playback stats (for mirroring, the phone's frame rate and network too), audio and subtitles. Back twice ends a cast; Home keeps it playing in the background.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <div class="icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3 4 6v6c0 5 3.5 8 8 9 4.5-1 8-4 8-9V6z"/><path d="m9 12 2 2 4-4"/></svg></div>
          <h3>Private by design</h3>
          <p>Choose who can cast: anyone, devices you allow on the TV, a PIN shown on the TV (as on an Apple TV) or a password. Block any device. No account, no data collection, fully open source.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="screens" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>See it on the TV</h2>
        <p>Screenshots from a Sony TV.</p>
      </div>
      <Screenshots lang="en" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="start">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>User guide</h2>
        <p>Three steps to your first cast. Your TV and phone need to be on the same network.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="card step">
          <h3>Install on your TV</h3>
          <p>Download the CastBay APK from GitHub and install it from a USB drive or the TV's file manager.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card step">
          <h3>Open CastBay</h3>
          <p>It starts receiving as soon as it opens, and from then on it's ready whenever the TV turns on.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card step">
          <h3>Cast from your phone</h3>
          <p>On an iPhone, choose Screen Mirroring in Control Center, or tap the AirPlay icon or cast button in an app, then pick "CastBay (your TV's name)".</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:28px"><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/guide">Read the full user guide</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Two ways to cast</h2>
        <p>Many apps support both; pick whichever suits you.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ways">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>AirPlay<span class="tag">Any app</span></h3>
          <ul>
            <li>From Control Center's Screen Mirroring, or an app's AirPlay icon</li>
            <li>Mirroring, music and video</li>
            <li>Lossless music; previous and next from the TV</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>DLNA<span class="tag">Apps' cast button</span></h3>
          <ul>
            <li>From the cast button inside video and music apps</li>
            <li>The TV plays it directly; lock your phone or walk away</li>
            <li>Works from iPhone and Android phones</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>FAQ</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Which TVs are supported?</summary>
          <p>Android TV and Google TV devices, TVs and boxes alike, on Android 8.0 or later. Tested on TCL (Android 9) and Sony (Android 12) TVs.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How do I install the APK on a TV?</summary>
          <p>Copy the APK to a USB drive, or open it with a file manager on the TV (ES File Explorer, for example). The first time, the TV may ask you to allow installing apps from unknown sources.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>My phone doesn't find CastBay. What now?</summary>
          <p>Make sure the TV and phone are on the same Wi-Fi and CastBay is open, waiting for a connection. Some routers enable "AP isolation", which stops devices finding each other; turn it off in the router's settings. Turning the phone's Wi-Fi off and on can also help.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does it support Google Cast (Chromecast) or Miracast?</summary>
          <p>No. Only Google-certified devices can receive Google Cast, and Miracast needs system-level access an app doesn't get. CastBay supports AirPlay and DLNA.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why is Bilibili limited to 720p?</summary>
          <p>Bilibili keeps 1080p and above for its own TV app; casting to other devices tops out at 720p. Use screen mirroring for 1080p.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I stop other people from casting to my TV?</summary>
          <p>Yes. In Settings → Connection → Casting verification, choose Confirm (the TV asks before a new device casts), PIN (a new device enters a PIN shown on the TV, once) or Password. Any device that has cast can be blocked there too.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I send music from a Mac?</summary>
          <p>Mac screen mirroring works. Music from the Mac's Music app doesn't: it uses an encryption the open-source AirPlay library CastBay is built on can't handle.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How do I stop a cast with the remote?</summary>
          <p>Press Back twice, or press Down for the quick menu and choose Stop casting. Pressing Home keeps the cast playing in the background.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why did changing a setting stop my cast?</summary>
          <p>Connection and screen-mirroring settings (the device name, casting verification, resolution and so on) only take effect when the receiver restarts, so the device that was casting has to connect again. Playback settings, such as picture fit, stats and lyrics, apply at once without interrupting anything.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why can't YouTube on my Android phone find CastBay?</summary>
          <p>YouTube for Android only casts with Google Cast, which only Google-certified devices can receive. Use the YouTube app on the TV itself, or cast from an iPhone or iPad, whose YouTube app uses AirPlay. Apps with a DLNA cast button (Bilibili, iQiyi, NetEase Cloud Music and others) work from Android phones.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I trust the APK I download?</summary>
          <p>Each release is built by GitHub from the tagged source, not on anyone's computer, and signed with CastBay's own key; the build checks the signature before publishing. The complete source of that exact version is attached to every release. Android also only installs an update over CastBay if it carries the same signature, so an altered copy can't replace the one you have.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does CastBay collect my data?</summary>
          <p>No. There's no account, analytics or ads. Only when you turn on lyrics does it send the song's title and artist to lrclib.net to look them up. See the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="alt">
    <div class="wrap oss">
      <div>
        <h2 style="margin:0;font-size:clamp(26px,3.2vw,34px)">Open source, and open to you</h2>
        <p>CastBay is open source under GPL-3.0, built on UxPlay, libplist, Apple's ALAC decoder and Media3 ExoPlayer. Issues and ideas are welcome on GitHub.</p>
      </div>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/weenas/castbay">github.com/weenas/castbay</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
</Page>
